From fe64a0cfeb2a6bf0f695d894e76984e107839118 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Magnus Lindholm <linmag7@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 12:49:48 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 07/10] sparc32: avoid duplicate publication of HyperSPARC
 highmem copies

copy_user_highpage() publishes the destination with __flush_page_to_ram()
and then immediately unmaps it. On HyperSPARC the highmem mapping always
uses a local fixmap slot, and both the narrow unmap path and its
conservative fallback synchronously publish data before kunmap_local()
returns.

Omit the first publication only for HyperSPARC highmem destinations when
the caller supplies a non-executable VMA. Keep the explicit operation for
lowmem, which can use the direct mapping and a no-op kunmap_local(), and
for other processors, executable VMAs and a NULL VMA.

Do not predict which unmap path will be selected: a recheck can fall back,
and both paths provide the needed publication. This depends on the
preceding highmem fixmap and publication-before-return changes.

Signed-off-by: Magnus Lindholm <linmag7@gmail.com>
---
 arch/sparc/mm/init_32.c | 10 +++++++++-
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/arch/sparc/mm/init_32.c b/arch/sparc/mm/init_32.c
index 59e55dd5c34e..4bb724575818 100644
--- a/arch/sparc/mm/init_32.c
+++ b/arch/sparc/mm/init_32.c
@@ -298,7 +298,15 @@ void copy_user_highpage(struct page *to, struct page *from,
 	vfrom = kmap_local_page(from);
 	vto = kmap_local_page(to);
 	copy_page(vto, vfrom);
-	__flush_page_to_ram((unsigned long)vto);
+	/*
+	 * HyperSPARC highmem mappings always use a local fixmap slot. Both
+	 * unmap paths publish its data before returning, so do not publish
+	 * twice. Keep the explicit operation for executable/unknown users
+	 * and for lowmem, where kunmap_local() can be a no-op.
+	 */
+	if (srmmu_modtype != HyperSparc || !PageHighMem(to) ||
+	    !vma || (vma->vm_flags & VM_EXEC))
+		__flush_page_to_ram((unsigned long)vto);
 	kunmap_local(vto);
 	kunmap_local(vfrom);
 }
